//OUT="[hello]"
//IN="hello"

// The input is the word "hello" with no trailing newline, so `read_str` hits
// end of input mid-word. `_builtin_getchar` is C's `getchar`, which yields -1
// there; the loop in `read_str` (src/core/io.cpl) has to stop on it and return
// the word it already read. It used to spin on -1 forever, pushing it into the
// string until the bump arena was exhausted, which made a program that asks for
// one more token than the input holds hang instead of answering.

fn main
    s = read_str()
    out "[{s}]"
